## Behaviour

### Code Standards
- Read `.forge/instructions/global.instructions.md` for domain-specific conventions
- Validate all suggestions against `.forge/conventions.json` (enforced by `forge lint`)
- Write failing tests before writing implementation code
- Prefer table-driven tests with `[]struct{ name, input, want }` shape
- `t.Parallel()` on every test; `t.TempDir()` for file fixtures
- Structured logging via `slog` — no `fmt.Println` in production paths

### Error Handling
- Always wrap errors: `fmt.Errorf("context: %w", err)`
- Use `errcode.New(ErrXxx, "detail", cause)` for user-facing CLI errors
- Never swallow errors silently

### Database / Migrations
- Migrations in `migrations/YYYYMMDDHHMMSS_description.sql`
- Every migration must have `-- migrate:up` and `-- migrate:down` sections
- Forward migrations must be additive only — no `DROP COLUMN` or `ALTER TYPE` on existing data
- RLS policies must be tested in `tests/security/`

### Security
- No secrets or tokens in source code — environment variables or secrets manager only
- Run `forge scan security` before any PR merge
- Consult `tests/security/` for existing RLS test patterns before adding new ones

### Forge Workflow
Before starting any feature:
1. `forge ship <feature>` to create a spec
2. Write tests (they must fail first)
3. Implement until tests pass
4. `forge scan security` + `forge lint` + `forge clean --check`

## File Map
```
.forge/instructions/global.instructions.md  — global LLM context
.forge/conventions.json                     — machine-readable conventions
migrations/                                 — timestamped SQL migrations
tests/security/                             — RLS + authz boundary tests
ROLLBACK.md                                 — rollback runbook
forge.config.yml                            — project config
```
